88kg\\n\\nInitiate at 1,000units/hr or 10mL/hr\\n\\n-aPTT monitoring: 6 hours after bolus/infusion started\\n-Goal aPTT: 60-80 seconds\\n-Titrate per protocol\"},{\"id\":\"1c46e231-1077-457c-8b62-5023edd84279\",\"medicationId\":\"11d86d68-7b9d-43d9-9d47-4bd583576860\",\"unitsOrdered\":0,\"frequency\":\"ONCE\",\"priority\":\"STAT\",\"indication\":\"NSTEMI\",\"orderingProvider\":\"Dr. Patel\",\"dose\":null,\"visibleInPresim\":false,\"status\":\"active\",\"instructions\":\"Bolus: 60units/kg IV once (max 4,000 units)\\nDosing weight: 88kg\\n\\nAdminister 4,000 units\"},{\"id\":\"d2dc693c-02c6-4377-b604-9021eb025364\",\"medicationId\":\"6616c6e3-dc8f-45bc-b3dc-1649ca65f1ef\",\"unitsOrdered\":0,\"frequency\":\"\",\"priority\":\"PRN\",\"indication\":\"Chest pain\",\"orderingProvider\":\"Dr. Reynolds\",\"dose\":\"0.4\",\"visibleInPresim\":true,\"status\":\"active\",\"instructions\":\"q5min PRN (max 3 doses) page provider if pain persists after 3 doses, hold for SBP less than 100\"},{\"id\":\"1103c67b-046f-4778-bb4d-14faefb7d0fe\",\"medicationId\":\"6f54bbbf-10a2-4c6f-8f78-1af77a32a7c6\",\"unitsOrdered\":0,\"frequency\":\"Q6H\",\"priority\":\"PRN\",\"indication\":\"HTN\",\"orderingProvider\":\"Dr. Reynolds\",\"dose\":\"25\",\"visibleInPresim\":false,\"status\":\"active\",\"instructions\":\"Give for SBP >160, hold if HR <60\"},{\"id\":\"8399a142-128a-4b68-abfd-15489f19e2c0\",\"medicationId\":\"5864e928-9af4-4371-af43-cbabddaa5193\",\"unitsOrdered\":0,\"frequency\":\"BID\",\"priority\":\"Routine\",\"indication\":\"HTN\",\"orderingProvider\":\"Dr. Patel\",\"dose\":\"10\",\"visibleInPresim\":true,\"status\":\"active\"},{\"id\":\"0a59330a-4f8e-4f12-8a0a-a0c3565ddba6\",\"medicationId\":\"6b2916b6-d81a-466a-b608-c78c219781bf\",\"unitsOrdered\":0,\"frequency\":\"QD\",\"priority\":\"Routine\",\"indication\":\"HLD\",\"orderingProvider\":\"Dr. Patel\",\"dose\":\"40\",\"visibleInPresim\":true,\"status\":\"active\"},{\"id\":\"96ba848a-a654-42ec-9e7b-d0ca0a170b97\",\"medicationId\":\"f17fdc0f-2c12-48cc-9b25-3e0ac850c336\",\"unitsOrdered\":0,\"frequency\":\"DAILY\",\"priority\":\"Routine\",\"indication\":\"\",\"orderingProvider\":\"Dr. Patel\",\"dose\":\"81\",\"visibleInPresim\":true,\"status\":\"active\"}],\"selectedMeds\":[{\"id\":\"4fb4a189-4fed-4382-b322-b681d142294f\",\"genericName\":\"morphine sulfate\",\"brandName\":\"Morphine IV\",\"route\":\"IV\",\"strength\":4,\"strengthUnit\":\"mg\",\"dispenseUnit\":\"Ampule\",\"isVariableDose\":false},{\"id\":\"37121d65-ac52-47f0-a381-fa96d426a8ca\",\"genericName\":\"heparin sodium\",\"route\":\"IV\",\"strength\":25000,\"strengthUnit\":\"units\",\"dispenseUnit\":\"Bag\",\"isVariableDose\":true,\"infusionRateUnit\":\"mL/hr\",\"diluent\":\"normal saline 0.9%\",\"totalVolume\":250},{\"id\":\"11d86d68-7b9d-43d9-9d47-4bd583576860\",\"genericName\":\"heparin sodium\",\"route\":\"IV\",\"strength\":10000,\"strengthUnit\":\"units\",\"dispenseUnit\":\"Vial\",\"isVariableDose\":true,\"totalVolume\":10},{\"id\":\"6616c6e3-dc8f-45bc-b3dc-1649ca65f1ef\",\"genericName\":\"nitroglycerin\",\"brandName\":\"Nitrostat\",\"route\":\"SL\",\"strength\":0.4,\"strengthUnit\":\"mg\",\"dispenseUnit\":\"Tab\",\"isVariableDose\":false},{\"id\":\"6f54bbbf-10a2-4c6f-8f78-1af77a32a7c6\",\"genericName\":\"metoprolol succinate\",\"brandName\":\"Toprol XL\",\"route\":\"PO\",\"strength\":25,\"strengthUnit\":\"mg\",\"dispenseUnit\":\"Tablet\",\"isVariableDose\":false},{\"id\":\"5864e928-9af4-4371-af43-cbabddaa5193\",\"genericName\":\"lisinopril\",\"brandName\":\"Zestril\",\"route\":\"PO\",\"strength\":10,\"strengthUnit\":\"mg\",\"dispenseUnit\":\"Tablet\",\"isVariableDose\":false},{\"id\":\"6b2916b6-d81a-466a-b608-c78c219781bf\",\"genericName\":\"atorvastatin\",\"brandName\":\"Lipitor\",\"route\":\"PO\",\"strength\":40,\"strengthUnit\":\"mg\",\"dispenseUnit\":\"Tablet\",\"isVariableDose\":false},{\"id\":\"f17fdc0f-2c12-48cc-9b25-3e0ac850c336\",\"genericName\":\"aspirin\",\"route\":\"PO\",\"strength\":81,\"strengthUnit\":\"mg\",\"dispenseUnit\":\"Tablet\",\"isVariableDose\":false}]},\"medicationAdministrations\":[{\"id\":\"b73bebf4-0b5e-4f6a-aee2-5a9676e2079b\",\"medicationOrderId\":\"28cf5d00-3f4b-4e0d-b74c-ab48db1bff2c\",\"administratorId\":\"Vanessa Adams, RN\",\"adminTimeMinuteOffset\":-240,\"status\":\"Given\",\"administeredDose\":2,\"visibleInPresim\":true},{\"id\":\"48db4589-3f3d-431a-b228-4989144ff213\",\"medicationOrderId\":\"d2dc693c-02c6-4377-b604-9021eb025364\",\"administratorId\":\"Vanessa Adams, RN\",\"adminTimeMinuteOffset\":-270,\"status\":\"Given\",\"administeredDose\":0.4,\"visibleInPresim\":true}]}"', 'Case Susan Dickson', '2026-06-23 03:52:13.327133+00', '2026-06-23 03:52:13.327133+00');
INSERT INTO "public"."clinical_documents" ("id", "case_id", "is_in_presim", "category", "specialty", "author", "time_offset", "doc_text", "created_at") VALUES
	('69b3ada8-6656-4199-9243-a433c6afc9b4', '79fcd248-1efd-4971-b370-d4bdfd69c734', true, 'History & Physical', 'Emergency Medicine', 'Dr. Regan Vanderwood', -2160, '<p><strong>History of Present Illness</strong></p><p>68-year-old male presents with progressive fatigue, dizziness, and dark tarry stools for 3 days. Reports worsening weakness today with near-syncope when standing. Mild epigastric discomfort. Denies vomiting blood. Denies chest pain.</p><p>States he takes apixaban for atrial fibrillation.</p><p>Concern for acute GI bleed with symptomatic anemia.</p><p><strong>Past Medical History</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>HTN</p></li><li><p>Type 2 DM</p></li><li><p>Atrial fibrillation</p></li><li><p>Peptic ulcer disease</p></li><li><p>Stage 3 CKD</p></li></ul><p><strong>Home Medications</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Lisinopril 10 mg daily</p></li><li><p>Metformin 1000 mg BID</p></li><li><p>Apixaban (Eliquis) 5 mg BID</p></li><li><p>Pantoprazole 40 mg daily</p></li><li><p>Ferrous sulfate</p></li></ul><p><strong>Review of Systems</strong></p><p>Positive:</p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Fatigue</p></li><li><p>Dizziness</p></li><li><p>Melena</p></li><li><p>Weakness</p></li></ul><p>Negative:</p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Hematemesis</p></li><li><p>Chest pain</p></li><li><p>SOB</p></li><li><p>Fever</p></li><li><p>Trauma</p></li></ul><p></p><p><strong>Physical Exam</strong></p><p><strong>General: </strong>Pale, fatigued appearing, slow to sit upright</p><p><strong>Cardiac: </strong>Tachycardic, irregular rhythm</p><p><strong>Respiratory: </strong>Clear bilaterally</p><p><strong>Abdomen: </strong>Soft, mild epigastric tenderness, no rebound</p><p><strong>Rectal: </strong>Black tarry stool, guaiac positive</p><p><strong>Skin: </strong>Cool, pale</p><p><strong>Neuro: </strong>Alert and oriented x4</p><p><strong>&nbsp;</strong></p><p><strong>ED Labs (Initial)</strong></p><p>Hgb 9.4, Hct 29, WBC 8.4, Plt 240, BUN 28, Cr 1.3, INR 1.6, K 3.6</p><p><strong>Imaging</strong></p><p>CT Abdomen/Pelvis: No perforation. Possible upper GI source</p><p><strong>ED Management</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>2 large-bore IVs placed</p></li><li><p>1L NS bolus</p></li><li><p>IV pantoprazole bolus + infusion</p></li><li><p>Type &amp; screen sent</p></li><li><p>Held apixaban</p></li><li><p>Serial CBCs ordered</p></li><li><p>GI consult requested</p></li></ul><p><strong>Medical Decision Making</strong></p><p>Presentation consistent with likely upper GI bleed given melena and elevated BUN. Occult stool positive. Hemodynamically borderline. At risk for further decline and anemia. Will admit to med-surg for monitoring, serial labs, and possible transfusion.</p>', '2026-06-17 18:45:18.391294+00'),
	('a68ccd8c-2e9a-461b-a9aa-1eb8941bc8af', '79fcd248-1efd-4971-b370-d4bdfd69c734', true, 'Nursing', 'Nursing', 'Tessa Ludwig, RN', -2040, '<p><strong>Chief Complaint:</strong><br>“I feel really weak and dizzy… and my stools have been black.”</p><p><strong>Initial Vitals</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Temp: 98.2°F (36.8°C)</p></li><li><p>HR: 104</p></li><li><p>BP: 102/64</p></li><li><p>RR: 18</p></li><li><p>SpO₂: 98% RA</p></li><li><p>Pain: 3/10 epigastric discomfort</p></li></ul><p><strong>Triage Assessment</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Pale appearance</p></li><li><p>Slow gait</p></li><li><p>Reports fatigue x 3–4 days</p></li><li><p>2 episodes near-syncope today</p></li><li><p>Black, tarry stools x 2 days</p></li><li><p>Denies hematemesis</p></li><li><p>On anticoagulant (Eliquis)</p></li></ul><p></p>', '2026-06-17 18:45:18.391294+00'),
	('191cb1f2-e8d4-4fa3-aba2-96eb53a36509', '2e66e8e8-8052-4561-bfb6-f59f3b4ac0fc', true, 'Admission', 'Emergency Medicine', 'Dr. Chen', -3000, '<p>Chief Complaint: “Vomiting and diarrhea for several days, feeling dizzy.”</p><p>History of Present Illness: 72-year-old male presenting after returning from a cruise 3 days ago. Reports persistent nausea, vomiting, and watery diarrhea since returning home. States he has been unable to tolerate solid food and has had minimal oral intake. Reports dizziness when standing and significant fatigue. Denies chest pain or shortness of breath. No blood in stool. Stool pathogen studies pending.</p><p>Past Medical History: Hypertension, GERD</p><p>Medications:</p><p>Lisinopril 10 mg daily</p><p>Omeprazole 20 mg daily</p><p></p><p>Allergies: No known drug allergies</p><p>Physical Examination:</p><p>General: Appears fatigued, mildly dehydrated</p><p>Neuro: Alert and oriented ×3</p><p>HEENT: Dry mucous membranes</p><p>Cardiac: Regular rate and rhythm</p><p>Respiratory: Clear breath sounds bilaterally</p><p>Abdomen: Soft, mild diffuse tenderness, hyperactive bowel sounds</p><p>Skin: Warm, decreased turgor</p><p></p><p>ED Vital Signs:</p><p>T: 37.6°C, HR: 90, BP: 116/70, RR: 18, SpO₂: 96% RA</p><p></p><p>ED Laboratory Results:</p><p>Na⁺ 125 mEq/L</p><p>K⁺ 3.7 mEq/L</p><p>Cl⁻ 94 mEq/L</p><p>BUN 25 mg/dL</p><p>Creatinine 1.1 mg/dL</p><p>WBC 11,000 /µL</p><p>Lactic acid 1.3 mmol/L</p><p></p><p>ED Treatment Provided:1 L Normal Saline IV bolus. Ondansetron 4 mg IV for nausea. Basic metabolic panel and CBC obtained. Patient monitored for several hours.</p><p>Assessment:</p><p>Hyponatremia and dehydration likely secondary to gastrointestinal illness. Patient stable but symptomatic with dizziness and electrolyte imbalance.</p><p></p><p>Plan:</p><p>Admit to medical-surgical floor for IV fluids, electrolyte monitoring, and observation.</p>', '2026-03-19 20:40:02.736559+00'),
	('3b0afdb8-0c2d-472e-86ef-4607ae276ded', '2e66e8e8-8052-4561-bfb6-f59f3b4ac0fc', true, 'Progress', 'Internal Medicine', 'Dr. David Adler', -1380, '<h2><u>Subjective</u></h2><p>&nbsp;Patient “feels warm” and reports worsening fatigue and nausea overnight. Appetite poor.</p><h2><u>Objective</u></h2><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Vitals: T 38.1, HR 104, BP 102/62, RR 22, SpO₂ 95%</p></li><li><p>Skin warm, flushed.</p></li><li><p>Urine output decreasing over past 12 hours.</p></li><li><p>Mild confusion noted when answering questions.</p></li><li><p>Na⁺ 129</p></li><li><p>BUN 28, Creatinine 1.2</p></li><li><p>WBC 13,500 (neutrophils 78%)</p></li><li><p>Lactic acid 1.7 (upper end of normal)</p></li></ul><h2><u>Assessment</u></h2><p>New fever with rising WBC and worsening fatigue → concern for developing infection. Electrolytes showing continued dehydration. Mentation slightly altered.</p><h2><u>Plan</u></h2><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Monitor vitals Q4 hours.</p></li><li><p>Encourage PO intake.</p></li><li><p>Repeat labs in AM.</p></li><li><p>Consider infectious workup if fever persists &gt;24 hours.</p></li><li><p>Continue maintenance IV fluids.</p></li></ul><p></p>', '2026-03-19 20:40:02.736559+00'),
	('731183a0-9329-4af0-a03d-190743a18b13', '2e66e8e8-8052-4561-bfb6-f59f3b4ac0fc', true, 'Consult', 'Physical Therapy', 'Mark LeGrande, PT', -2400, '<h2><u>Subjective</u></h2><p>&nbsp;Patient reports “feeling weak” and slightly dizzy when standing.</p><h2><u>Objective</u></h2><p>Transfers: required minimal assistance.</p><p>Gait: unsteady initially.<br>Orthostatic vitals during session:</p><p>Supine BP 116/74 → standing BP 102/68<br>HR increased from 90 to 104<br>Patient reported lightheadedness.</p><h2><u>Assessment</u></h2><p>Orthostatic hypotension likely related to dehydration. Activity limited due to dizziness.</p><h2><u>Plan</u></h2><p>&nbsp;Recommend short sessions only; re-evaluate in 24–48 hours. Encourage slow positional changes.</p>', '2026-03-19 20:40:02.736559+00'),
	('a44db1fc-cf42-4c17-a55f-5fe54fb7e532', '2e66e8e8-8052-4561-bfb6-f59f3b4ac0fc', true, 'Progress', 'Internal Medicine', 'Dr. Adler', -2880, '<h2><u>Subjective</u></h2><p>Patient presented with 3 days of nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea following return from cruise. Reports dizziness, fatigue, and poor oral intake.</p><h2><u>Objective</u></h2><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Vitals: T 37.2°C, HR 88, BP 118/72, RR 18, SpO₂ 97% RA</p></li><li><p>Dry mucous membranes, skin tenting noted.</p></li><li><p>Alert and oriented ×3.</p></li><li><p>Abdomen soft, slightly tender, hyperactive bowel sounds.</p></li><li><p>Na⁺ 125 mEq/L, K⁺ 3.7 mEq/L</p></li><li><p>BUN 25 mg/dL, Creatinine 1.1 mg/dL</p></li><li><p>WBC 11,000/µL</p></li><li><p>Lactic acid 1.3 mmol/L</p></li></ul><h2><u>Assessment</u></h2><p>&nbsp;Hyponatremia likely secondary to fluid losses. Mild dehydration. Hemodynamically stable.</p><h2><u>Plan</u></h2><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Start NS 75 mL/hr.</p></li><li><p>Advance diet as tolerated.</p></li><li><p>Monitor electrolytes every AM.</p></li><li><p>Fall precautions for dizziness.</p></li><li><p>PT/OT evaluation due to weakness.</p></li></ul><p></p>', '2026-03-19 20:40:02.736559+00'),
	('c88a2dab-e487-41ce-8a8a-5bdd500b3b44', '2e66e8e8-8052-4561-bfb6-f59f3b4ac0fc', true, 'Consult', 'Occupational Therapy', 'Susan Bower, OT', -960, '<h2><u>Subjective</u></h2><p>Patient states: “I feel weaker today.”</p><h2><u>Objective</u></h2><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Difficulty maintaining balance while sitting and standing.</p></li><li><p>Increasing fatigue with minimal activity.</p></li><li><p>Orthostatic symptoms persist: BP drop from 108/66 sitting → 94/60 standing</p></li></ul><h2><u>Assessment</u></h2><p>Reduced functional tolerance. Potential early infection contributing to increased fatigue</p><h2><u>Plan</u></h2><p>Hold therapy until vital signs stabilize.</p>', '2026-03-19 20:40:02.736559+00'),
	('3982f7ba-8418-4d73-ad82-8e9952a5e87b', '2e66e8e8-8052-4561-bfb6-f59f3b4ac0fc', true, 'Nursing', 'Nursing', 'Barbara Gifford, RN', -240, '<p>Assessment:<br>Patient lethargic, responds slowly to questions. Skin hot and flushed. Continues to report weakness and “feeling off.” Mucous membranes dry.<br></p><p>Vital Signs: T 39.0, HR 118, BP 90/56, RR 24, SpO₂ 93%.</p><p></p><p>Concerns:</p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Urine output only 80 mL since midnight.</p></li><li><p>Patient increasingly confused.</p></li><li><p>Tachycardia and hypotension worsening.</p></li></ul><p></p><p>Actions: Notified physician, applied cool compresses, encouraged oral fluids but patient unable to tolerate.</p>', '2026-03-19 20:40:02.736559+00'),
	('d588719f-365e-4bb8-a9c5-2ba3dae33714', '2e66e8e8-8052-4561-bfb6-f59f3b4ac0fc', true, 'Nursing', 'Nursing', 'Derrick Williams, RN', -960, '<p>Assessment:<br>Patient restless but cooperative. Skin warm, flushed. Mucous membranes remain dry. Slight confusion—incorrectly reported date once. Complains of “feeling hot.”</p><p></p><p>Vital Signs:<br> T 38.4, HR 110, BP 96/58, RR 22, SpO₂ 94% RA.<br></p><p>Physical Findings:</p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Cap refill 3–4 sec</p></li><li><p>Urine output 150 mL over last 4 hours</p></li><li><p>Lungs clear but diminished at bases<br></p></li></ul><p>Cooling measures applied. Notified physician of hypotension and mental status change. Provided oral fluids; patient tolerated small amounts. Physician aware; will reassess.</p>', '2026-03-19 20:40:02.736559+00'),
	('da2358d3-63c8-43b6-a1c8-eba16d678ae2', '2e66e8e8-8052-4561-bfb6-f59f3b4ac0fc', true, 'Nursing', 'Nursing', 'Jane Smith RN', -2520, '<p>Assessment:<br>Patient awake, alert, cooperative. Complains of fatigue and mild dizziness on standing. Mucous membranes dry. Skin warm and slightly flushed.</p><p>Vital Signs:<br>T 37.6, HR 92, BP 114/70, RR 20, SpO₂ 96% RA.</p><p></p><p>Interventions:</p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Encouraged fluids.</p></li><li><p>Assisted patient to bathroom with standby assistance.</p></li><li><p>Noted concentrated urine.</p></li><li><p>Notified physician of continued dizziness upon standing.</p></li></ul><p></p><p>Response:<br> Patient tolerated interventions; continued monitoring planned.</p>', '2026-03-19 20:40:02.736559+00'),
	('3cde7e61-6bf8-44a4-8051-97e2498dacce', 'ad7f3138-3312-425a-aeac-578db75c7c43', true, 'Progress', 'Internal Medicine', 'Dr. Adler', -120, '<h2><u>Subjective:</u> </h2><h2>Patient presented with 3 days of nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea following return from cruise. Reports dizziness, fatigue, and poor oral intake.</h2><p></p><h2><u>Objective:</u> </h2><h2>Vitals: T 37.2 C, HR 88, BP 118/72, RR 18, SpO2 97% RA</h2><h2>Dry mucous membranes, skin tenting noted</h2><h2>Alert and oriented x3</h2><h2>Abdomen soft, slightly tender, hyperactive bowel sounds</h2><h2>Labs:</h2><h2>Na+ 125mEq/L</h2><h2>K+ 3.7 mEq/L</h2><h2>BUN 25 mg/dL, Creatinine 1.1 mg/dL</h2><h2>WBC 11,000</h2><h2>Lactic acid 1.3 mmol/L</h2><p></p><h2><u>Assessment:</u></h2><h2>Hyponatremia likely secondary to fluid losses. Mild dehydration. Hemodynamically stable.</h2><p></p><h2><u>Plan:</u></h2><h2>Start NS 75mL/hr</h2><h2>Advance diet as tolerated</h2><h2>Monitor electrolytes every AM</h2><h2>Fall precautions for dizziness</h2><h2>PT/OT eval due to weakness</h2><p></p>', '2026-05-11 16:55:54.47202+00'),
	('657003be-ec9e-4048-93e5-23e2bdb9bb88', 'ad7f3138-3312-425a-aeac-578db75c7c43', true, 'Nursing', 'Nursing', 'Jane Smith, RN', -240, '<h2><u>Situation:</u></h2><h2>Mr. Adams is a 72-year-old male with nausea/vomiting x3days, hypotension, and hyponatremia</h2><h2></h2><h2><u>Background:</u></h2><h2>History of GERD and HTN. Vomiting started after return from cruise.</h2><h2></h2><h2><u>Assessment:</u></h2><h2>Orthostatic hypotension noted:</h2><h2>Lying BP: 112/68 HR 98</h2><h2>Sitting BP: 100/60 HR 108</h2><h2>Standing BP: 92/58 HR 118</h2><h2>Dry mucous membranes, poor skin turgor, decreased urine output, Na+ 125</h2><p></p><h2><u>Recommendation:</u></h2><h2>Continue fluids</h2><h2>Reassess orthostatics post-fluid challenge</h2><h2>Maintain contact precautions for GI rule out</h2><h2>Consider provider update for sodium trend and standing BP</h2><p></p>', '2026-05-11 16:55:54.47202+00'),
	('6f6b4e39-3f0a-4b29-a73a-e2fac800ef4e', 'ad7f3138-3312-425a-aeac-578db75c7c43', true, 'Admission', 'Emergency Medicine', 'Dr. Chen', -360, '<p>Chief Complaint: “Vomiting and diarrhea for several days, feeling dizzy.”</p><p></p><p>History of Present Illness: 72-year-old male presenting after returning from a cruise 3 days ago. Reports persistent nausea, vomiting, and watery diarrhea since returning home. States he has been unable to tolerate solid food and has had minimal oral intake. Reports dizziness when standing and significant fatigue. Denies chest pain or shortness of breath. No blood in stool. Stool pathogen studies pending.</p><p></p><p>Past Medical History: Hypertension, GERD</p><p></p><p>Medications:</p><p>Lisinopril 10 mg daily</p><p>Omeprazole 20 mg daily</p><p></p><p>Allergies: No known drug allergies</p><p></p><p>Physical Examination:</p><p>General: Appears fatigued, mildly dehydrated</p><p>Neuro: Alert and oriented ×3</p><p>HEENT: Dry mucous membranes</p><p>Cardiac: Regular rate and rhythm</p><p>Respiratory: Clear breath sounds bilaterally</p><p>Abdomen: Soft, mild diffuse tenderness, hyperactive bowel sounds</p><p>Skin: Warm, decreased turgor<br></p><p>ED Vital Signs:</p><p>T: 37.6°C, HR: 90, BP: 116/70, RR: 18, SpO₂: 96% RA</p><p></p><p>ED Laboratory Results:</p><p>Na⁺ 125 mEq/L</p><p>K⁺ 3.7 mEq/L</p><p>Cl⁻ 94 mEq/L</p><p>BUN 25 mg/dL</p><p>Creatinine 1.1 mg/dL</p><p>WBC 11,000 /µL</p><p>Lactic acid 1.3 mmol/L<br></p><p>ED Treatment Provided: 1 L Normal Saline IV bolus. Ondansetron 4 mg IV for nausea. Basic metabolic panel and CBC obtained. Patient monitored for several hours.</p><p></p><p>Assessment:</p><p>Hyponatremia and dehydration likely secondary to gastrointestinal illness. Patient stable but symptomatic with dizziness and electrolyte imbalance.<br></p><p>Plan:</p><p>Admit to medical-surgical floor for IV fluids, electrolyte monitoring, and observation.</p>', '2026-05-11 16:55:54.47202+00'),
	('110244f9-29a6-45f7-a949-738d92532db7', '3aa3d373-0368-4d66-90dd-d3c1b530347e', true, 'Admission', 'Emergency Medicine', 'Dr. Hans', -60, '<h2>Patient presented with severe epigastric and right upper quadrant pain- onset after eating burger and fries. Reported nausea and one episode of vomiting. No current nausea. Oxycodone PRN for pain. Awaiting RUQ ultrasound. NPO status until further diagnosis.</h2><h2>ED vital signs: T 37.6 C, HR 102, BP 140/88, RR20, SpO2 97% RA, Pain 8/10.</h2><p></p>', '2026-05-12 03:45:08.533935+00'),
	('da961e09-2cda-43d5-9826-a8b384fe29ef', '9c4d050f-f9bc-47aa-a0b3-461c12dd5337', true, 'Admission', 'Emergency Medicine', 'Dr. Chen', -60, '<h2>Patient presented with shortness of breath and weight gain over the past week. States he missed several doses of his water pill.</h2><h2><u>Assessment: </u></h2><h2>+lower extremity swelling</h2><h2>+crackles lower lobes bilaterally</h2><h2>+JVD</h2><h2>ED Vital Signs: T 37.0 C, HR 112, BP 158/72, RR 24, SpO2 92% 2L nc</h2><h2>Plan- </h2><h2>Titrate oxygen to maintain saturation 92% or greater</h2><h2>Administer PO lasix now- consider IV lasix pending lab results and kidney function</h2><h2>Resume home blood pressure medications</h2><h2>Fluid restriction</h2><h2>Monitor observation status</h2><p></p>', '2026-05-12 15:21:12.416794+00'),
	('991c4f67-83da-49bc-a8da-9394498d3f7f', '79fcd248-1efd-4971-b370-d4bdfd69c734', true, 'Nursing', 'Nursing', 'Sierra Johnson, RN', -1920, '<p>Patient admitted to inpatient unit. Continues to report weakness and lightheadedness. Skin pale and cool. IV fluids infusing. No active vomiting. Education provided on NPO status and fall precautions.</p>', '2026-06-17 18:45:18.391294+00'),
	('7cf6191a-9d50-4cc5-bff2-361276daf69f', '79fcd248-1efd-4971-b370-d4bdfd69c734', true, 'Progress', 'Internal Medicine', 'Alex Reynolds, MD', -1680, '<p><strong>Subjective: </strong>Patient reports ongoing fatigue and intermittent dizziness. Denies hematemesis but continues to have black stools. Mild epigastric discomfort persists. No chest pain or shortness of breath.</p><p><strong>Objective:</strong></p><p>Vital Signs:</p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Temp: 98.4°F</p></li><li><p>HR: 102</p></li><li><p>BP: 100/62</p></li><li><p>RR: 18</p></li><li><p>SpO₂: 98% RA</p></li></ul><p><strong>Physical Exam:</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>General: Pale, fatigued</p></li><li><p>Cardiac: Irregularly irregular rhythm</p></li><li><p>Abdomen: Mild epigastric tenderness, no guarding</p></li><li><p>Skin: Cool, pale</p></li><li><p>Neuro: A&amp;O x4</p></li></ul><p><strong>Labs Reviewed</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Hgb: 8.6 → trending downward</p></li><li><p>BUN elevated (suggestive of GI bleed)</p></li><li><p>INR mildly elevated (on anticoagulant)</p></li></ul><p><strong>Assessment</strong></p><ol class="list-decimal ml-10" type="1"><li><p>Suspected upper GI bleed</p></li><li><p>Symptomatic anemia (developing)</p></li><li><p>Atrial fibrillation on anticoagulation</p></li><li><p>CKD stage 3</p></li></ol><p><strong>Plan</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Serial CBC q8h</p></li><li><p>Continue IV pantoprazole infusion</p></li><li><p>Hold apixaban</p></li><li><p>IV fluids</p></li><li><p>GI consult for EGD</p></li><li><p>Monitor for signs of hemodynamic instability</p></li><li><p>Fall precautions</p></li></ul><p></p>', '2026-06-17 18:45:18.391294+00'),
	('6e333de1-f23b-4bce-b99e-acc3c6147a4f', '79fcd248-1efd-4971-b370-d4bdfd69c734', true, 'Consult', 'Gastroenterology', 'Amir Patel, MD', -1560, '<p><strong>Reason for Consult: </strong>Melena and suspected upper GI bleed</p><p><strong>HPI: </strong>68-year-old male with history of peptic ulcer disease and anticoagulation presents with melena and symptomatic anemia. Progressive fatigue and orthostatic symptoms.</p><p><strong>Assessment</strong></p><p>High suspicion for <strong>upper GI bleed</strong>, likely:</p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Peptic ulcer disease (most likely)</p></li><li><p>Possible anticoagulation-related bleeding</p></li></ul><p><strong>Plan</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Urgent EGD within 24 hours</p></li><li><p>Continue IV pantoprazole infusion</p></li><li><p>Hold anticoagulants</p></li><li><p>Maintain hemodynamic stability</p></li><li><p>Transfuse PRN</p></li><li><p>NPO</p></li></ul><p></p>', '2026-06-17 18:45:18.391294+00'),
	('96820b88-50be-45b1-98ee-80831c165c50', '79fcd248-1efd-4971-b370-d4bdfd69c734', true, 'Nursing', 'Nursing', 'Sierra Johnson, RN', -1440, '<p>Patient reports weakness and shortness of breath. Dark stool x2. Lightheaded with ambulation.</p>', '2026-06-17 18:45:18.391294+00'),
	('2a8ea336-5540-457f-8d76-612cea39f1cc', '79fcd248-1efd-4971-b370-d4bdfd69c734', true, 'Progress', 'Internal Medicine', 'Alex Reynolds, MD', -1200, '<p><strong>Subjective:</strong> Patient reports worsening weakness and dizziness. States he feels “lightheaded when standing.” Reports continued dark stools overnight.</p><p><strong>Objective:</strong></p><p>Vital Signs:</p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>HR: 110</p></li><li><p>BP: 94/58</p></li><li><p>RR: 20</p></li></ul><p><strong>Physical Exam:</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Increased pallor</p></li><li><p>Orthostatic hypotension present</p></li><li><p>Abdomen unchanged</p></li></ul><p><strong>Labs</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Hgb: 8.6 → 7.4</p></li><li><p>BUN rising</p></li><li><p>K decreasing</p></li></ul><p><strong>Assessment</strong></p><ol class="list-decimal ml-10" type="1"><li><p>Active upper GI bleed – ongoing blood loss</p></li><li><p>Worsening anemia</p></li><li><p>Volume depletion</p></li><li><p>Electrolyte imbalance (hypokalemia)</p></li></ol><p><strong>Plan</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Continue IV PPI infusion</p></li><li><p>Replace potassium</p></li><li><p>Maintain IV fluids</p></li><li><p>Type &amp; crossmatch</p></li><li><p>Prepare for EGD today</p></li><li><p>Anticipate need for transfusion if Hgb &lt;7 or symptomatic- blood consent obtained and in patient chart</p></li></ul><p></p>', '2026-06-17 18:45:18.391294+00'),
	('4cb1be4a-6f5b-4719-8d9f-a967a2705109', '79fcd248-1efd-4971-b370-d4bdfd69c734', true, 'Post-op', 'Gastroenterology', 'Amir Patel, MD', -960, '<p><strong>Esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD)</strong><br><strong>Endoscopist:</strong> Amir Patel, MD</p><p><strong>Indication: </strong>Melena and symptomatic anemia</p><p><strong>Findings</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p><strong>Actively bleeding gastric ulcer</strong> (lesser curvature)</p></li><li><p>Visible vessel present</p></li><li><p>No esophageal varices</p></li><li><p>No perforation</p></li></ul><p><strong>Interventions Performed</strong></p><ol class="list-decimal ml-10" type="1"><li><p>Epinephrine Injection</p></li></ol><p>o Injected into and around ulcer base</p><p>o Purpose: vasoconstriction to reduce bleeding</p><ol class="list-decimal ml-10" start="2" type="1"><li><p>Thermal Coagulation (Cautery)</p></li></ol><p>o Applied to bleeding vessel</p><p>o Purpose: achieve hemostasis</p><ol class="list-decimal ml-10" start="3" type="1"><li><p>Hemostatic Clip Placement</p></li></ol><p>o Clip placed over visible vessel</p><p>o Purpose: mechanically stop bleeding</p><p><strong>Outcome</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Bleeding controlled</p></li><li><p>No immediate complications</p></li><li><p>Patient tolerated procedure well</p></li></ul><p><strong>Post-Procedure Orders</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Continue IV PPI infusion (72 hrs)</p></li><li><p>NPO initially → advance diet</p></li><li><p>Monitor for re-bleeding:</p></li></ul><p>o melena</p><p>o hematemesis</p><p>o dropping Hgb</p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Serial CBCs</p></li></ul><p>Avoid NSAIDs/anticoagulants</p>', '2026-06-17 18:45:18.391294+00'),
	('874fa346-af47-4692-9002-57300dfe765e', '79fcd248-1efd-4971-b370-d4bdfd69c734', true, 'Progress', 'Gastroenterology', 'Amir Patel, MD', -720, '<p>Patient underwent EGD. Bleeding gastric ulcer identified and treated.</p><p><strong>Assessment Update</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Source of bleed confirmed: <strong>gastric ulcer</strong></p></li><li><p>Hemostasis achieved endoscopically</p></li><li><p>Still high risk for re-bleed</p></li></ul><p><strong>Plan</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Continue PPI infusion x 72 hours</p></li><li><p>NPO → advance diet per GI</p></li><li><p>Monitor Hgb closely- 7.2</p></li><li><p>Transfuse PRBCs if symptomatic or Hgb &lt;7</p></li><li><p>Continue telemetry</p></li><li><p>Colonoscopy planned for full evaluation</p></li></ul><p></p>', '2026-06-17 18:45:18.391294+00'),
	('07ab1ad4-bd62-4d0e-a2f5-135e6a47d2b7', '79fcd248-1efd-4971-b370-d4bdfd69c734', true, 'Progress', 'Internal Medicine', 'Alex Reynolds, MR', -240, '<p><strong>Assessment:</strong><br>68 y/o male with upper GI bleed secondary to gastric ulcer. Progressive symptomatic anemia. Orthostatic and tachycardic. Hgb 6.9.</p><p><strong>Plan:</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Transfuse 2 units PRBCs</p></li><li><p>Monitor closely for transfusion reaction</p></li><li><p>Continue IV pantoprazole infusion</p></li><li><p>Hold anticoagulation</p></li><li><p>Colonoscopy tomorrow</p></li><li><p>Maintain K &gt; 3.5</p></li><li><p>Strict I&amp;O</p></li></ul><p></p>', '2026-06-17 18:45:18.391294+00'),
	('8ad53bb6-7478-43d6-8015-27e384c1af54', '37b3b78d-44cc-4391-8d6a-8d93dda40387', true, 'Consult', 'Cardiology', 'Dr. Reynolds', -240, '<p>CARDIOLOGY CONSULT NOTE</p><p><strong>Consult History:</strong></p><p>Patient evaluated in emergency department at request of emergency medicine provider for persistent chest pain concerning for possible cardiac etiology.</p><p>Patient reports onset of midsternal chest pressure earlier today while at home. Pain described as pressure-like sensation with radiation into upper back. Initial pain severity reported at 7/10, currently improved to 4/10 after administration of sublingual nitroglycerin.</p><p>Associated symptoms include mild nausea and indigestion sensation. Patient denies dyspnea, diaphoresis, syncope, or palpitations.</p><p>Significant cardiac risk factors include hypertension, hyperlipidemia, former heavy smoking history, and family history of coronary artery disease.</p><p><strong>Cardiac Review:</strong></p><p>ECG reviewed. Sinus rhythm present without ST elevation. Mild non-specific T wave abnormality noted in inferior leads.</p><p>Initial troponin I reviewed at 0.03 ng/mL, borderline but non-diagnostic.</p><p>Patient remains hemodynamically stable at time of evaluation.</p><p><strong>Assessment:</strong></p><p>Chest pain concerning for possible unstable angina versus evolving non-ST elevation myocardial infarction.</p><p>Current workup remains inconclusive, however patient has several significant cardiovascular risk factors requiring continued cardiac monitoring and serial biomarker evaluation.</p><p>Low suspicion for STEMI at present.</p><p><strong>Recommendations:</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Continue telemetry monitoring</p></li><li><p>Trend troponin q3 hours</p></li><li><p>Repeat 12-lead ECG with recurrent chest pain or clinical change</p></li><li><p>Continue aspirin therapy</p></li><li><p>Nitroglycerin PRN chest discomfort</p></li><li><p>Consider initiation of beta blocker therapy if blood pressure remains elevated</p></li><li><p>Maintain NPO status pending possible stress testing versus cardiac catheterization if troponin elevates</p></li><li><p>Cardiology to continue following</p></li></ul><p><strong>Impression:</strong></p><ol class="list-decimal ml-10"><li><p>Chest Pain suspicious for Acute Coronary Syndrome</p></li><li><p>Rule Out NSTEMI</p></li><li><p>Possible unstable angina</p></li><li><p>Multiple cardiac risk factors present</p></li></ol><p></p><p>Electronically Signed<br>Michael Reynolds, MD<br>Cardiology</p>', '2026-06-23 03:50:49.942267+00'),
	('dedc8a18-e977-4d8d-bec8-599fd6eb9f02', '37b3b78d-44cc-4391-8d6a-8d93dda40387', true, 'History & Physical', 'Emergency Medicine', 'Dr. Samuel Johnson', -270, '<p><strong>Chief Complaint:</strong> Chest pain</p><p>Ms. Dickson is an adult female presenting to the emergency department with complaints of persistent substernal chest pressure that began several hours prior to arrival while at home performing normal daily activities. Patient describes discomfort as a pressure sensation located mid-sternum with radiation to the upper back. Pain initially rated 7/10 and has remained persistent despite rest.</p><p>Patient reports associated mild nausea and indigestion sensation. Denies shortness of breath, diaphoresis, dizziness, syncope, palpitations, fever, recent illness, cough, or lower extremity swelling.</p><p>Given persistent symptoms and concern for possible cardiac etiology, patient presented to ED for evaluation.</p><p>Past medical history: HTN, HLD, GERD</p><p>Home medications: </p><p>-Lisinopril 10mg BID</p><p>-Omeprazole 20mg daily</p><p>-Atorvastatin 20mg daily</p><p>Patient states she took all home medications this morning prior to arrival to ED.</p><p>Family history: Father died from MI at age 60, mother HTN and breast CA</p><p>Social history: former smoker, quit 2 years ago (2packs/day), social alcohol use, denies recreational drug use</p><p><strong>Vital Signs:</strong></p><p>BP: 148/92 mmHg</p><p>HR: 96 bpm</p><p>RR: 20/min</p><p>Temp: 98.4F</p><p>SpO2: 96% room air</p><p><strong>Physical Exam:</strong></p><p>General: Alert, oriented x4, mildly uncomfortable secondary to chest pain.</p><p>Cardiac: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs appreciated. Peripheral pulses intact.</p><p>Respiratory: Lungs clear bilaterally. No respiratory distress.</p><p>GI: Abdomen soft, non-tender, non-distended.</p><p>Skin: Warm, dry, no diaphoresis.</p><p>Neurologic: No focal deficits noted.</p><p><strong>Diagnostic Results</strong>:</p><p>12-Lead ECG:<br>Normal sinus rhythm. No ST elevation. Non-specific T wave inversion noted in lead III. No STEMI criteria met.</p><p>Initial Troponin I: 0.03 ng/mL<br>(Lab cutoff 0.04 ng/mL)</p><p>BMP/CMP: Within normal limits</p><p>Chest X-Ray: No acute cardiopulmonary abnormalities</p><p><strong>ED Interventions:</strong></p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Cardiac monitor initiated</p></li><li><p>Peripheral IV established</p></li><li><p>Aspirin 325 mg PO administered</p></li><li><p>Nitroglycerin 0.4 mg SL x1 administered</p></li><li><p>Morphine 2mg IV once</p></li><li><p>Cardiology consulted due to persistent chest pain with abnormal ECG findings</p></li></ul><p>Differential includes:</p><p>-Acute Coronary Syndrome / NSTEMI</p><p>-Unstable Angina</p><p>-GERD exacerbation</p><p>-Esophageal spasm</p><p>-Musculoskeletal chest pain</p><p><strong>Plan:</strong></p><p>Admit to telemetry observation.</p><p>Orders placed for:</p><ul class="list-disc ml-6"><li><p>Continuous telemetry monitoring</p></li><li><p>Nitroglycerin PRN chest pain</p></li><li><p>Metoprolol tartrate 25 mg PO PRN SBP &gt;160</p></li><li><p>Cardiology consultation</p></li><li><p>NPO pending further workup</p></li></ul><p></p><p>Electronically Signed<br>Samuel Johnson, MD<br>Emergency Medicine</p>', '2026-06-23 03:50:49.942267+00');
